conflict in high-income countries.

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:42.690
He received his PhD in politics

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:45.040
and social policy from Princeton

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.380
and will be an assistant
professor of political science

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:51.410
and political administration
at the University

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:56.410
of North Carolina at Charlotte
starting in August of 2021.

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.860
And I do want to recognize
the non-presenting authors

00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:06.350
who are not gonna be making
a presentation tonight

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:09.970
but contributed very much to the paper.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:14.230
Frances Rosenbluth is
the Damon Wells Professor

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:16.170
of Political Science at Yale,

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:19.073
a fellow of the American
Academy of Arts and Sciences,

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:26.040
a leading scholar of
comparative political economy

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.540
and a beloved colleague.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:34.900
Her current work focuses on
the electoral microfoundations

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:37.470
of different forms of
capitalism on the politics

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.400
of gender inequality.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.200
She published many articles on,

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.380
on a variety of subjects,

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.320
some recently on the
political economy of gender

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:50.840
and on women's
under-representation in politics.

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:53.467
Her recent book with Ian Shapiro was

00:05:53.467 --> 00:05:57.150
"Responsible Parties: Saving
Democracy from Itself",

00:05:57.150 --> 00:05:59.810
published in 2018.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:03.993
And then the fifth author on
this paper is Maikol Cerda.

00:06:05.283 --> 00:06:08.300
Cerda is a postdoctoral
fellow at Yale University,

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:11.103
received a PhD in economics from NYU.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:15.480
Cerda's research interests
include industrial organization,

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:18.520
competition policy, statistical learning

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:20.313
and applied microeconomics.

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:23.603
So those are our paper authors.

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.441
I'll finally conclude
with our two discussants.

00:06:28.441 --> 00:06:30.510
Our two discussants are Frances Lee

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:32.380
and Jonathan Rodden.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:37.180
Frances Lee is a professor of
politics and public affairs,

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:40.960
an associate chair at the
department of politics,

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:42.820
Princeton University,

00:06:42.820 --> 00:06:45.573
fellow of the American
Academy of Arts and Sciences.

00:06:46.436 --> 00:06:48.930
Her research centers on American politics

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:52.130
with a special focus on
congressional politics,

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:54.090
national policymaking,

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:56.720
party politics and representation.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:59.660
She is the editor of the
Cambridge Elements series

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:01.700
in American politics

00:07:01.700 --> 00:07:04.870
and her recent book "Insecure Majorities:

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:07.959
Congress and the Perpetual
Campaign" explore the rise

00:07:07.959 --> 00:07:11.760
of congressional party
conflict showing how shifts

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:16.260
in competitiveness had a
profound impact on how Democrats

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:18.823
and Republicans interact
in the policy sphere.

00:07:19.790 --> 00:07:22.700
Lee is the recipient of the highest prizes

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:24.753
for her scholarship

00:07:24.753 --> 00:07:27.620
from the American Political
Science Association.

00:07:27.620 --> 00:07:30.030
She received the Richard Fenno Prize

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:32.840
for the best book in legislative studies

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:35.010
and she is a two-time recipient

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:37.380
of the D.B. Hardeman Prize,

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:40.080
presented by the Lyndon
Baines Johnson Foundation

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:43.280
for the best book on the US Congress

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:45.640
from the fields of biography, history,

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:48.000
journalism and political science.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:53.000
I'll just say that there's
no one who is a finer scholar

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:56.410
of the contemporary
Congress who is actively

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:59.950
writing now than, than Professor Lee.

00:07:59.950 --> 00:08:03.863
It is a true delight to
have her with us tonight.

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:06.107
Jonathan Rodden.

00:08:07.502 --> 00:08:09.010
Jonathan Rodden is a professor

00:08:09.010 --> 00:08:11.990
of political science
at Stanford University.

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:14.360
He is a senior fellow at
the Hoover Institution

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:18.440
and the Stanford Institute
for Economic Policy Research.

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:19.730
He is the founder and director

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:22.690
of the Stanford Spatial
Social Science Lab,

00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:24.130
the center for research and teaching,

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:29.130
focusing on geospatial analysis
in the social sciences.

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:31.950
That's a subject that Jonathan is

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:35.141
among the world's experts on that subject,

00:08:35.141 --> 00:08:38.223
the geospatial analysis of politics.

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:41.220
Rodden's interests lie at the intersection

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.720
of political science, public
economics and geography.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.870
He's the author of a number of books

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:51.600
including a 2007 book,
"Hamilton's Paradox:

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.750
The Promise and Peril
of Fiscal Federalism"

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:56.780
and for which he was the recipient

00:08:56.780 --> 00:09:00.590
of the Gregory Luebbert
Prize for the best book

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:03.520
in comparative politics in that year.

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:05.120
Again, thank you, Jonathan.

00:09:05.120 --> 00:09:06.230
Welcome back to Yale.

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:11.230
Jonathan was a student
here a number of years ago

00:09:11.690 --> 00:09:14.210
and it's wonderful to have you back.

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:17.040
So with that, let me turn it over to,

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:19.890
I believe Ian is gonna go first.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:22.050
Let me turn it over to Ian.

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:22.883
After Ian

00:09:24.182 --> 00:09:27.360
and Akhil and Alexander are done,

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:31.160
we'll go immediately to
Frances and then Jonathan,

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:33.490
the panelists will have their discussion

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:37.030
and then we'll go immediately
to question and answer.

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:39.133
Thank you all for joining us this evening.

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:42.580
- Thank you, Alan

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:46.410
and thank you, Pam and more for

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:50.930
doing all the heavy lifting
to get us here this evening.

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:52.367
Thank you also to Frances

00:09:52.367 --> 00:09:55.630
and Jonathan for agreeing to do this.

00:09:55.630 --> 00:09:56.940
We very much appreciate it

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:58.790
and we look forward to your comments.

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:01.390
I'm just gonna talk briefly

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:04.980
about the project out
of which this paper came

00:10:04.980 --> 00:10:09.980
and then I will turn it
over to Akhil and then Alex

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:13.433
to present different parts
of this particular paper.

00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:17.780
As Alan mentioned, this work comes out

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:20.689
of the Leitner Program
on Effective Democratic

00:10:20.689 --> 00:10:24.500
Governance at the Jackson Institute

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:27.750
that Frances Rosenbluth and I run,

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:31.310
and we should take note
that all of the research

00:10:31.310 --> 00:10:36.180
here is paid for by Jim
Leitner through that program

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:39.573
and we wouldn't be able to
do it but for his generosity.

00:10:41.258 --> 00:10:42.660
The remit

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:44.720
of the

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:46.510
program is to study

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:49.190
which types of democracy
are most conducive

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:52.840
to good public policy and
within democratic systems

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:55.960
as we find them as we've
inherited them if you like,

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:58.470
what institutional
reforms would would make

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:00.570
them operate more effectively

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:03.070
and produce better public policy?

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:04.963
This is the work that,

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:08.150
this one stream of this work

00:11:08.150 --> 00:11:10.917
that Alex, Michael, Akhil

00:11:10.917 --> 00:11:13.400
and I have been doing with Frances

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:15.730
that has produced several papers

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:18.260
and is producing several more

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:21.470
and they were all focused in one way

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:24.630
or another on the

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:28.910
organization and governance
of political parties

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:32.270
and how that affects political outcomes.

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:37.240
In the US, this has led us
to devote a lot of attention

00:11:37.240 --> 00:11:40.530
to the study of the
operation and evolution

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:42.230
of the primary system,

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:43.830
of course at the presidential level

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:48.670
that's only been since the
1970s when they became important

00:11:48.670 --> 00:11:52.610
but at the congressional
levels, as everyone knows,

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:55.440
primaries have been around
since the progressive era,

00:11:55.440 --> 00:12:00.440
so it's not just primaries
that have gotten our attention

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:05.640
but rather the interaction
between primaries and safe seats

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:08.120
and we have built up a
data set on the growth

00:12:08.120 --> 00:12:10.080
of safe seats that they're going

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:12.660
to be talking about tonight.

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:15.760
And so we've been looking
at that more generally

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:18.250
as it relates to things
like party discipline

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:22.870
and the capacity to produce
policies that are good

00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:25.280
for the economy in the longer run.

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:28.610
This particular research
comes at the intersection

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:32.870
between that and Frances
Rosenbluth's ongoing interest

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:34.850
in female representation

00:12:34.850 --> 00:12:37.730
and so that was bringing those two streams

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:41.934
of interests together
that resulted in the paper

00:12:41.934 --> 00:12:44.230
that we're gonna talk about tonight.

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:47.720
So with that, let me
turn it over to Akhil.

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:48.893
The floor is yours.

00:12:50.415 --> 00:12:51.248
- Great.

00:12:51.248 --> 00:12:52.220
Thank you, Professor Shapiro.

00:12:52.220 --> 00:12:54.223
Let me just load those slides up.

00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:58.130
So thanks everyone for coming out today.

00:12:58.130 --> 00:13:00.120
So as professor Shapiro was saying,

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:00.953
we're gonna be looking

00:13:00.953 --> 00:13:03.160
at the problem of the rise of safe seats

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:03.993
in the United States

00:13:03.993 --> 00:13:06.560
and how that's gonna help us understand

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:09.420
this growing phenomenon of
women's under-representation

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:11.000
in the US Congress.

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:12.210
But before I dive into that,

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:14.120
I wanna start with a brief roadmap

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:16.430
for where we're gonna go
in our presentation today.

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:17.960
So I'm going to start
by talking a little bit

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:20.730
about the problem and the
conventional explanations

00:13:20.730 --> 00:13:23.170
for this gender gap between the parties

00:13:23.170 --> 00:13:25.890
in women's representation in Congress,

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:27.540
then we're gonna shift over to talking

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:29.900
about our story contrary
to these conventional

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:33.480
explanations that safe seats are to blame.

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:35.350
Alex is gonna hop on and talk

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:38.260
about the connection between
safe seats and extremism

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:39.430
and then we're gonna loop back

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:43.410
to this question of safe seats
and women's representation.

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:45.180
We'll have a little bit of a discussion

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:47.840
and questions for
further scholarly inquiry

00:13:47.840 --> 00:13:50.080
and then we're excited
to get some feedback

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:53.083
from our wonderful discussants here today.

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:57.180
So to just ground us in
the idea of the problem,

00:13:57.180 --> 00:13:59.550
it's well-documented
across political science

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:01.700
and also across popular media

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:04.050
that women are dramatically
underrepresented

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:05.700
in the United States Congress.

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:08.920
But what is sometimes less
appreciated is the trajectory

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:11.260
of how things are changing.

00:14:11.260 --> 00:14:12.440
Within the Democratic Party,

00:14:12.440 --> 00:14:16.100
we are seeing substantial
gains for women politicians

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:18.170
especially in the House
of Representatives,

00:14:18.170 --> 00:14:20.430
slightly less fewer gains in the Senate,

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:22.270
but among Republicans,
we're actually seeing

00:14:22.270 --> 00:14:23.540
very anemic growth

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:24.820
and one way to look at that is

00:14:24.820 --> 00:14:28.730
in the partisan gap in women
members of the US House.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:33.340
So if you go all the way back
to 1985 to the 99th Congress,

00:14:33.340 --> 00:14:34.770
there was no partisan gap,

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:37.540
there was an equal
number of women and men,

00:14:37.540 --> 00:14:39.690
sorry, of women elected
from the Democratic

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:41.330
and the Republican Party,

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:44.210
versus today you see a huge gap of upwards

00:14:44.210 --> 00:14:46.570
of 60 seats between the two of them,

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:48.730
most famously we saw this in 2018

00:14:48.730 --> 00:14:51.420
which was heralded as
the year of the woman

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:54.280
for the record number of women
were elected to Congress,

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:57.310
but actually, if you
look closer at the data,

00:14:57.310 --> 00:15:00.750
a record number of Republican
women lost their seats.

00:15:00.750 --> 00:15:02.670
So when we're talking
about this gender gap

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:03.860
in representation,

00:15:03.860 --> 00:15:05.280
it's also important that we look

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:08.653
at the growing partisan gender
gap as one of the sources.

00:15:10.220 --> 00:15:13.060
Now, the conventional
explanations are twofold.

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:15.330
First, some point to
the partisan gender gap

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:16.210
among the electorate.

00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:19.820
It's true that we see these
same patterns reflected

00:15:19.820 --> 00:15:20.840
in the electorate.

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:22.770
Women are more likely
to vote for Democrats

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:24.450
and men, on average, are more likely

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:25.930
to vote for Republicans.

00:15:25.930 --> 00:15:27.610
And the second explanation is, of course,

00:15:27.610 --> 00:15:30.080
the under-supply of
Republican women candidates,

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:33.800
which is somewhat related
to that first explanation.

00:15:33.800 --> 00:15:35.130
But there are a couple of reasons why

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:39.230
we think both of those explanations
fall a little bit short.

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:42.290
So the first, looking at the
partisan gap in the electorate,

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:44.500
we see that it's always been high.

00:15:44.500 --> 00:15:48.660
Dating back to 1980, you had
almost a nine point gender gap

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:50.750
and today, if you're looking at exit polls

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:53.480
in presidential voting,
which as unreliable they are,

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:55.650
offer us probably the best metric

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:57.650
for intertemporal comparison,

00:15:57.650 --> 00:16:00.130
that gap has risen to about 11 points.

00:16:00.130 --> 00:16:02.040
So it is true that you could argue

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:04.040
that the gender gap has gotten wider,

00:16:04.040 --> 00:16:05.980
but perhaps not as significantly

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:09.133
among the electorate as it has
among elected representatives

00:16:09.133 --> 00:16:10.610
in Congress.

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:12.510
So to put that more concretely,

00:16:12.510 --> 00:16:16.240
the gender gap since 1985
in Congress has risen

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:18.940
from zero to 60 seats between the parties,

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:20.520
among the electorate, it's increased

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:22.870
by a maximum of about four points.

00:16:22.870 --> 00:16:25.160
And the other thing to
note is that in contrast

00:16:25.160 --> 00:16:27.860
to our last slide which
showed this steady rise

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:29.770
in the gender gap between the Democrats

00:16:29.770 --> 00:16:32.050
and Republicans in Congress,

00:16:32.050 --> 00:16:33.980
the swings in the electorate haven't had

00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:37.950
either the drama in terms of
magnitude, but also direction.

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:41.560
We saw times in this period
between 1980 and 2016

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:43.870
when most scholarly accounts would agree,

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:45.260
actually the gender gap among

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:47.660
the electorate got less severe

00:16:47.660 --> 00:16:49.230
and yet the gender gap among members

00:16:49.230 --> 00:16:51.813
of Congress has increased steadily.

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:56.150
The second explanation would
be that undersupply story,

00:16:56.150 --> 00:16:59.531
that there just aren't enough
women Republicans running

00:16:59.531 --> 00:17:01.710
but there are two problems with this.

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:03.610
The first is that an undersupply story

00:17:03.610 --> 00:17:06.620
itself is arguably endogenous.

00:17:06.620 --> 00:17:08.810
You can't construct a
complete causal story

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:11.260
and the reason is something
we'll get to later

00:17:11.260 --> 00:17:12.180
in our presentation,

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:15.050
which is if you say that
there are voter biases

00:17:15.050 --> 00:17:16.560
against women candidates

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:18.480
and women candidates don't run

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:21.140
because they're aware
of those voter biases,

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:23.210
can you really say the undersupply is

00:17:23.210 --> 00:17:25.180
the complete causal story

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:27.530
for why we have women's
under-representation?

00:17:27.530 --> 00:17:29.280
We think that that's a flawed argument

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:31.920
or, at best, an incomplete argument.

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:34.530
And then the other reason
that we are skeptical

00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:35.830
of this conventional account is

00:17:35.830 --> 00:17:37.210
that it's unclear why this problem

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:40.010
of undersupply should have
increased in magnitude

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:41.310
over the past years,

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:43.920
despite the fact that candidate access

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:46.670
to the ballot has been greatly
democratized over the years.

00:17:46.670 --> 00:17:48.810
We see more candidates running

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:51.890
and therefore we should see
arguably less in gender barriers

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:53.480
to candidacy as well.

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:56.570
So for those two reasons, we
enter this project skeptical

00:17:56.570 --> 00:17:57.990
of the conventional narratives

00:17:57.990 --> 00:18:01.373
around the gender gap within Congress.

00:18:03.060 --> 00:18:05.690
So our story is focused

00:18:05.690 --> 00:18:08.760
on the electoral institutions
that create these

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:12.840
gender biases in representation
and specifically safe seats.

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:14.710
So we see the partisan gap

00:18:14.710 --> 00:18:18.000
as a function of declining
electoral competition.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:20.530
We provide evidence and
we hypothesize initially

00:18:20.530 --> 00:18:22.780
that women candidates
are gonna outperform men

00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:24.110
in Democratic safe seats,

00:18:24.110 --> 00:18:26.170
but crucially they're
gonna underperform men

00:18:26.170 --> 00:18:28.300
in Republican safe seats

00:18:28.300 --> 00:18:29.570
and we'd say that because of that,

00:18:29.570 --> 00:18:31.450
the recent rise in safe seats,

00:18:31.450 --> 00:18:33.150
the exponential growth of safe seats,

00:18:33.150 --> 00:18:36.640
among both parties can help
contribute to that explanation

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:39.520
of why this partisan gap
has grown over the period

00:18:39.520 --> 00:18:41.403
of time we're studying.

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:44.700
The prior literature really builds

00:18:44.700 --> 00:18:47.760
to that hypothesis in three separate ways.

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:50.810
So the first is there's a wide body

00:18:50.810 --> 00:18:53.360
of evidence about how women
candidates are stereotyped

00:18:53.360 --> 00:18:56.990
as more liberal than men
that goes back into the 1990s

00:18:56.990 --> 00:18:58.970
but with more recent additions,

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:01.680
like Sanbonmatsu and Dolan in 2009,

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:05.200
seems to be a pattern that
is still well and alive.

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:07.200
We also see some evidence
in the literature

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:10.400
that safe seats are intensifying
the importance of primaries

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:13.920
and thereby extremism
because the primary mode

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:16.560
of competition is no longer
this general election

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:19.390
where politicians are supposedly competing

00:19:19.390 --> 00:19:20.530
for the median voter

00:19:20.530 --> 00:19:22.490
but rather the primary
where they're competing

00:19:22.490 --> 00:19:24.080
for the median primary voter.

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:26.630
And we're also gonna present
some evidence in addition

00:19:26.630 --> 00:19:30.350
to this prior literature for
why we think that effect holds

00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:33.250
and why we think these safe
seats encourage extremisms.

00:19:33.250 --> 00:19:35.520
And then the final thing
we're going to talk

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:38.778
about is a combination
of both literature on how

00:19:38.778 --> 00:19:41.620
safe seats have been on the
rise over the past decade

00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:44.030
and also a new data set we've put together

00:19:44.030 --> 00:19:46.990
to document precisely
how much they have risen

00:19:46.990 --> 00:19:49.340
over this period of time.

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:51.920
So with that, I'm gonna
turn it over to Alex

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:55.883
to preview our arguments about
safe seats and extremism.

00:19:58.480 --> 00:19:59.313
- Yeah.

00:19:59.313 --> 00:20:00.146
Hi, everyone.

00:20:00.146 --> 00:20:00.979
Thanks, Akhil.

00:20:00.979 --> 00:20:02.280
So I just wanted to kind of elaborate

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:04.080
on these last two points that you had

00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:06.810
about the importance of safe
seats and their increase

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:11.000
and this is a part of our
broader project on, you know,

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:14.150
the role of electoral
competition and party governance

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:16.350
but I think it's very
relevant to today's paper.

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:18.850
Also I just wanted to spend
a few minutes on that.

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:24.870
So first, you know, I
just wanna kind of present

00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:27.470
this very kind of basic graph which plots,

00:20:27.470 --> 00:20:29.940
you know, the ideology of
a certain representative

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:31.543
alongside their, you know,

00:20:31.543 --> 00:20:35.150
with the partisan composition
of their constituency

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:37.280
and, you know, this graph basically shows

00:20:37.280 --> 00:20:40.720
that in places with more Democrats,

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:42.850
you have more liberal representatives

00:20:42.850 --> 00:20:44.840
in terms of the voting patterns

00:20:44.840 --> 00:20:47.200
and in places with more, you know,

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:49.310
Republicans you have representatives

00:20:49.310 --> 00:20:51.410
who vote more conservatively,
quote, unquote,

00:20:51.410 --> 00:20:53.380
as measured by DW-NOMINATE score

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:55.470
which of course we know
has certain limitations

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:58.050
but we can also show that this picture is

00:20:58.050 --> 00:20:59.880
pretty much staying the same

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:02.021
regardless of what measure you use,

00:21:02.021 --> 00:21:02.854
whether it's CF scores,

00:21:02.854 --> 00:21:05.500
DW-NOMINATE score or some
kind of interest group scores.

00:21:05.500 --> 00:21:08.070
And, you know, oftentimes
when you see this picture,

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:09.500
I think the first thing that pops up

00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:11.660
which we fully knowledge
is that, you know,

00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:14.610
if you take a given district
with a certain composition,

00:21:14.610 --> 00:21:17.070
let's say a district with, you know,

00:21:17.070 --> 00:21:19.170
more or less even part of the composition

00:21:19.170 --> 00:21:20.400
of Democrats or Republicans, you know,

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:21.740
depending on whether it's represented

00:21:21.740 --> 00:21:22.800
by a Democrat or Republican,

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:26.020
the DW-NOMINATE score or the
ideology of representative

00:21:26.020 --> 00:21:28.540
and their awarding patterns
can be very different

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:30.938
and it's true but what
we also see here is that,

00:21:30.938 --> 00:21:33.630
you know, it is also true that depending

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:36.030
on the partisan composition
of a certain district,

00:21:36.030 --> 00:21:37.410
whether it's a safe Democratic seats

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:39.390
or a safe Republican seats,

00:21:39.390 --> 00:21:40.223
the voting patterns

00:21:40.223 --> 00:21:42.463
of representatives are
very much different.

00:21:42.463 --> 00:21:43.296
And, you know,

00:21:43.296 --> 00:21:45.210
we don't wanna kind of
have any normative ideas

00:21:45.210 --> 00:21:47.180
about whether it's bad or good,

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:48.500
but I think it's also important

00:21:48.500 --> 00:21:50.780
to have some kind of comparative glance

00:21:50.780 --> 00:21:54.260
and think about whether it's
truly need to be the case.

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:57.140
So for instance, if we can
consider not just the US

00:21:57.140 --> 00:21:59.100
but kinda other, you know,

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:00.980
two-party democracies,

00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:03.420
then we can also see that
actually it doesn't have

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:05.130
to be the case that, you know,

00:22:05.130 --> 00:22:08.240
a place with a more, you know,

00:22:08.240 --> 00:22:10.550
like a safer constituency
for a certain party,

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:13.130
it has to have a very
different voting patterns.

00:22:13.130 --> 00:22:17.040
You know, if the party has
control or candidates and,

00:22:17.040 --> 00:22:19.680
you know, kinda
institutions are different.

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:21.840
So why is it the case that, you know,

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:24.950
safer seats produce
more extreme candidates?

00:22:24.950 --> 00:22:27.210
So there are three main reasons for that,

00:22:27.210 --> 00:22:29.380
that, you know, kind
of we wanna emphasize.

00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:31.817
You know, first and most
obviously is that, you know,

00:22:31.817 --> 00:22:33.940
obviously if you have a
very safe Democratic seats

00:22:33.940 --> 00:22:37.460
that probably it is the case
that on average, you know,

00:22:37.460 --> 00:22:40.000
it has more extreme Democratic voters

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:41.167
and so, you know,

00:22:41.167 --> 00:22:42.920
the representatives are gonna

00:22:44.189 --> 00:22:46.943
responding to this more extreme voters.

00:22:47.830 --> 00:22:51.470
An issue more kind of alluded this story

00:22:51.470 --> 00:22:52.470
than you might think

00:22:53.371 --> 00:22:55.120
and for instance, you know,
Jonathan Rodden were discussing

00:22:55.120 --> 00:22:57.020
has a very good evidence on that,

00:22:57.020 --> 00:22:58.640
that you can easily imagine

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:02.040
that you can have a
majority Democratic district

00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:03.440
that most have voted for Biden,

00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:05.060
but it's actually not
that extreme in terms

00:23:05.060 --> 00:23:06.130
of its voting population,

00:23:06.130 --> 00:23:08.650
but it's still the case
that on average, of course,

00:23:08.650 --> 00:23:10.780
you know, they have safe
Democratic seats is gonna be

00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:12.610
more extreme in terms
of the borders there.

00:23:12.610 --> 00:23:14.390
It's also the case that when you have

00:23:14.390 --> 00:23:16.220
a safe democratic seats,

00:23:16.220 --> 00:23:17.630
in terms of campaign finance,

00:23:17.630 --> 00:23:20.810
there are gonna be more demand
for outside money there.

00:23:20.810 --> 00:23:22.510
And it is interesting because sometimes

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:24.010
we don't really kind of consider

00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:25.880
that you have a safe
Democratic seats is actually

00:23:25.880 --> 00:23:28.150
it still requires a lot of, you know,

00:23:28.150 --> 00:23:30.990
funds and money to actually
compete in those places

00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:35.150
but it is the case that in those
places we see more reliance

00:23:35.150 --> 00:23:39.090
on interest groups and
kind of outside money

00:23:39.090 --> 00:23:39.923
in terms of, you know,

00:23:39.923 --> 00:23:42.160
out of district spending as well.

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:44.320
But most importantly
for our purposes here,

00:23:44.320 --> 00:23:47.100
I would like to emphasize
that in safer seats,

00:23:47.100 --> 00:23:49.390
whether it's Democratic
or Republican seats,

00:23:49.390 --> 00:23:53.100
we have more vulnerability
to more primary challengers

00:23:53.100 --> 00:23:54.870
and also more extreme primary challenges.

00:23:54.870 --> 00:23:55.920
And I just wanted to show you

00:23:55.920 --> 00:23:58.030
like a few graphs that can be relevant

00:23:58.030 --> 00:23:59.950
to what we're talking about here.

00:23:59.950 --> 00:24:02.870
Right, so first, if we
just consider the universe

00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:05.460
of primary challengers

00:24:05.460 --> 00:24:07.210
and, you know, the incumbents who have

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:08.470
to face these challenges,

00:24:08.470 --> 00:24:11.330
we see that the primary
challengers are usually

00:24:11.330 --> 00:24:14.370
more ideologically extreme
as measured as CF scores

00:24:14.370 --> 00:24:18.381
here which is based on
campaign finance networks.

00:24:18.381 --> 00:24:19.500
So what we see here is
basically when we look

00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:22.690
at challengers that are blue dots here,

00:24:22.690 --> 00:24:26.410
they on average, you know,
a higher in this matlibplots

00:24:26.410 --> 00:24:27.920
which means they have kind of more

00:24:27.920 --> 00:24:30.390
conservative, quote,
unquote, ideology compared

00:24:30.390 --> 00:24:32.230
to the incumbents in those districts.

00:24:32.230 --> 00:24:34.550
And we see that kind of
opposite picture for that

00:24:34.550 --> 00:24:35.383
for Democrats.

00:24:35.383 --> 00:24:38.610
So we see that if we look
at Democratic challengers

00:24:38.610 --> 00:24:40.110
in safer seats,

00:24:40.110 --> 00:24:42.640
or more generally they're
usually more extreme

00:24:42.640 --> 00:24:45.413
than those that are incumbents.

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:48.860
But most importantly, for our purposes,

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:51.480
we also see that if you're in a safe seat,

00:24:51.480 --> 00:24:55.100
which is measured by this
Cook Partisan Voting Index

00:24:55.100 --> 00:24:58.150
that Akhil is gonna talk a
little bit more about later,

00:24:58.150 --> 00:25:01.200
we see that, you know, representatives

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:04.240
and incumbents in those
districts are more likely

00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:05.940
to face primary challenge

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:09.823
and actually even more so the
case in Republican safe seats.

00:25:11.660 --> 00:25:13.960
And now they're kind of part of the puzzle

00:25:13.960 --> 00:25:15.390
which is important for our story here

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:17.920
which is part of the paper is

00:25:17.920 --> 00:25:20.380
that we see a gradual decline

00:25:20.380 --> 00:25:22.530
in electoral competition

00:25:22.530 --> 00:25:26.549
for US House across here.

00:25:26.549 --> 00:25:29.110
So, you know, obviously
we have redistricting,

00:25:29.110 --> 00:25:33.563
we have sort of like
oscillations here and there

00:25:33.563 --> 00:25:34.950
but in general, the trend is on decline,

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:36.782
so this is just, you know,

00:25:36.782 --> 00:25:41.110
seat safety based on, you
know, basic margin of victory

00:25:41.110 --> 00:25:43.480
but we can also see the
same story when it comes

00:25:43.480 --> 00:25:46.410
to kinda more ex-ante measure
of competitiveness based

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:49.010
on this Cook Partisan Voting Index

00:25:49.010 --> 00:25:51.260
where we see that there is a decline,

00:25:51.260 --> 00:25:54.140
has been in decline of
marginal seats since 1992,

00:25:54.140 --> 00:25:56.020
since, you know, when we had the data

00:25:56.020 --> 00:25:59.360
and we also have a slight
increase of Democratic

00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:01.663
and especially Republican safe seats.

00:26:03.566 --> 00:26:06.830
Okay, Akhil, I think I
can go back to you now.

00:26:06.830 --> 00:26:07.663
Thank you.

