00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:12.990
So yeah, so connecting
the story of safe seats

00:26:12.990 --> 00:26:15.270
and extremism back to our original theory,

00:26:15.270 --> 00:26:18.960
what we posit then is that
Democratic women should perform

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:20.800
better in safe Democratic seats,

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:24.030
whereas Republican women should
be dramatically outperformed

00:26:24.030 --> 00:26:25.603
in safe Republican seats.

00:26:26.440 --> 00:26:30.460
And so to really study this
problem in a more rigorous way,

00:26:30.460 --> 00:26:32.780
we combine two existing data sets

00:26:32.780 --> 00:26:34.870
and opine some metrics

00:26:34.870 --> 00:26:37.140
that we think are lacking in them so far.

00:26:37.140 --> 00:26:40.640
So first we start with
Adam Bonica's 2019 database

00:26:40.640 --> 00:26:42.770
of ideology and money in elections

00:26:42.770 --> 00:26:45.660
to identify all of the
candidates who have filed

00:26:45.660 --> 00:26:48.040
with the FEC to run for Congress

00:26:48.040 --> 00:26:50.980
and some basic demographic
information of them,

00:26:50.980 --> 00:26:52.510
both on the campaign finance side

00:26:52.510 --> 00:26:55.870
and also personal demographic
information like gender

00:26:55.870 --> 00:26:58.310
and the nice thing about
this data set is we also

00:26:58.310 --> 00:27:01.520
get a sense of their
actual electoral fortunes.

00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:05.650
Then we take a dataset
from Cook Political to look

00:27:05.650 --> 00:27:06.780
at the cook PVI,

00:27:06.780 --> 00:27:08.970
which is a measure of
the partisan composition

00:27:08.970 --> 00:27:12.240
on electoral competitiveness
of house districts.

00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:16.180
So the way to think of a Cook
PVI is as a relative measure.

00:27:16.180 --> 00:27:19.280
So if we say a district
is Democrat plus five,

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:21.090
that means in a year where Democrats

00:27:21.090 --> 00:27:24.090
and Republicans tied
overall across the nation,

00:27:24.090 --> 00:27:27.010
we would expect that district
to have voted five points more

00:27:27.010 --> 00:27:30.333
for the Democratic candidate
than the Republican candidate.

00:27:32.900 --> 00:27:34.580
And the first thing that we try to do is

00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:36.880
just a pretty basic descriptive task.

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:40.570
So right here what we see is
the modeled win probability

00:27:40.570 --> 00:27:45.370
for the Democratic and
Republican candidates

00:27:45.370 --> 00:27:48.140
by their seat safety and by gender.

00:27:48.140 --> 00:27:50.310
So just to walk us through
this really quickly,

00:27:50.310 --> 00:27:53.590
the top panels are all Republicans

00:27:53.590 --> 00:27:55.330
and what we see here is that,

00:27:55.330 --> 00:27:58.470
looking at the leftmost panel
which is the overall win rate,

00:27:58.470 --> 00:28:02.230
for the vast majority of seats,
you see almost gender parody

00:28:02.230 --> 00:28:04.830
in that the probability that a woman

00:28:04.830 --> 00:28:08.540
or a man who enters the
primary election has virtually

00:28:08.540 --> 00:28:10.420
the same probability of
winning the election.

00:28:10.420 --> 00:28:12.640
Now I should caution
that these are probably

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:15.720
conservative estimates
of gender bias because

00:28:15.720 --> 00:28:18.700
as Anzia and Berry have
very famously shown,

00:28:18.700 --> 00:28:21.840
women candidates who actually
enter elections are often

00:28:21.840 --> 00:28:24.640
of higher quality than
their male counterparts.

00:28:24.640 --> 00:28:26.260
But the important takeaway here is

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:27.740
that even though you have gender parody

00:28:27.740 --> 00:28:29.370
for the vast majority of seats,

00:28:29.370 --> 00:28:34.280
you see divergence among these
very safe seats on the right

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:36.614
and you might think, well,
how many seats can it be

00:28:36.614 --> 00:28:38.980
that are more than R+20?

00:28:38.980 --> 00:28:40.850
How big of a problem this is,

00:28:40.850 --> 00:28:43.210
actually it's a fairly
substantial problem.

00:28:43.210 --> 00:28:47.050
In this current Congress,
about 1/4 of all seats are

00:28:47.050 --> 00:28:49.240
in that range where we see that divergence

00:28:49.240 --> 00:28:52.620
on the Republican side
and on the Democratic side

00:28:52.620 --> 00:28:55.470
you also see a slightly stark pattern

00:28:55.470 --> 00:28:56.730
in the opposite direction,

00:28:56.730 --> 00:28:59.000
which is that in the
safe Democratic seats,

00:28:59.000 --> 00:29:02.160
women are advantaged in their
overall likelihood of winning.

00:29:02.160 --> 00:29:04.930
Again, this is conditional
on them entering the primary,

00:29:04.930 --> 00:29:06.050
which suggests they may be

00:29:06.050 --> 00:29:09.080
of higher quality than
their male challengers.

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:11.350
But as you get into
more competitive seats,

00:29:11.350 --> 00:29:13.170
you see that advantage fade away

00:29:13.170 --> 00:29:16.340
and you actually see some
evidence of a disadvantage.

00:29:16.340 --> 00:29:19.560
This is all to say that from
a purely descriptive account,

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:23.580
it does seem as though women
candidates are advantaged

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:25.490
in the Democratic side in safer seats

00:29:25.490 --> 00:29:28.760
and disadvantaged on the
Republican side in safer seats

00:29:28.760 --> 00:29:31.320
and that gender gap we showed does seem

00:29:31.320 --> 00:29:33.513
to originate partially in safer seats.

00:29:34.570 --> 00:29:37.810
But when we add slightly
more rigorous methods

00:29:37.810 --> 00:29:40.840
and control for other potential
confounding variables,

00:29:40.840 --> 00:29:43.170
does relationship still hold up?

00:29:43.170 --> 00:29:45.760
Our answer, at least
as far as we've tested,

00:29:45.760 --> 00:29:47.350
it seems to be yes,

00:29:47.350 --> 00:29:50.593
that as you get more extreme seats,

00:29:51.970 --> 00:29:53.520
the relationship right here,

00:29:53.520 --> 00:29:56.870
this our fourth variable
that we have listed,

00:29:56.870 --> 00:29:58.020
female times PVI,

00:29:58.020 --> 00:29:59.370
that's the interaction effect

00:29:59.370 --> 00:30:01.810
between your seat getting safer

00:30:01.810 --> 00:30:04.550
in the conservative direction
and a woman candidate.

00:30:04.550 --> 00:30:08.080
So these estimates would
suggest in very liberal seats

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:10.620
you should see, which
would have negative PVIs,

00:30:10.620 --> 00:30:12.470
you'd see an advantage to being a woman

00:30:12.470 --> 00:30:14.547
versus in very conservative seats

00:30:14.547 --> 00:30:16.930
you would see a stark disadvantage

00:30:16.930 --> 00:30:20.270
which supports the descriptive
finding that we had

00:30:20.270 --> 00:30:21.933
in the last slide.

00:30:23.550 --> 00:30:25.390
And so now we wanna return back to one

00:30:25.390 --> 00:30:29.630
of the important age
alternative hypotheses

00:30:29.630 --> 00:30:31.090
that we presented at the beginning

00:30:31.090 --> 00:30:33.160
that the conventional
explanation literature holds

00:30:33.160 --> 00:30:34.520
which is that, you know,

00:30:34.520 --> 00:30:37.390
part of this reason that you
see that stark relationship

00:30:37.390 --> 00:30:39.440
between seat safety

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:42.410
and women candidates
could be candidate supply.

00:30:42.410 --> 00:30:44.240
And we do find actually evidence

00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:46.290
that candidate supply starkly differs

00:30:46.290 --> 00:30:48.000
between Democrats and Republicans,

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:49.880
but what's important to us is we also see

00:30:49.880 --> 00:30:51.510
that that relationship differs

00:30:51.510 --> 00:30:52.570
in terms of seat safety,

00:30:52.570 --> 00:30:55.440
that yes, you have that overall decline,

00:30:55.440 --> 00:30:56.650
that overall partisan gap.

00:30:56.650 --> 00:30:58.207
excuse me, in women who run

00:30:58.207 --> 00:31:01.870
but as seats trend towards
safer Republican seats.

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:05.320
less and less women are running
for Congress, which suggests

00:31:05.320 --> 00:31:09.100
that this safe seat
explanation cannot be explained

00:31:09.100 --> 00:31:13.480
simply by the overall raw
partisan gender gap itself

00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:15.320
and because of that, we think that this is

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:17.600
an important domain for future research

00:31:17.600 --> 00:31:20.890
and an important way of
understanding why we've gotten

00:31:20.890 --> 00:31:24.530
to a point where the dynamics
of Congress are such that,

00:31:24.530 --> 00:31:27.560
you know, women Democrats are on the rise

00:31:27.560 --> 00:31:30.630
but women Republicans have
seen pretty anemic growth

00:31:32.229 --> 00:31:34.960
since the 1980s

00:31:34.960 --> 00:31:37.510
and one thing that I just
wanna mention before we move

00:31:37.510 --> 00:31:41.090
on to the discussion is a
potential challenge for our case

00:31:41.090 --> 00:31:43.170
one would think could
be this most recent set

00:31:43.170 --> 00:31:44.880
of elections, 2020.

00:31:44.880 --> 00:31:47.200
of course, famously in 2020,

00:31:47.200 --> 00:31:49.700
almost every single
Republican who defeated

00:31:49.700 --> 00:31:53.260
an incumbent Democrat was a
woman or a candidate of color

00:31:53.260 --> 00:31:54.560
but actually when we go through

00:31:54.560 --> 00:31:55.940
and look at the the evidence,

00:31:55.940 --> 00:31:58.050
we do see further evidence for our theory

00:31:58.050 --> 00:32:00.710
in that it was only in
these competitive seats

00:32:00.710 --> 00:32:03.200
that the Republican women were able to win

00:32:03.200 --> 00:32:05.370
and actually they made very little headway

00:32:05.370 --> 00:32:07.915
in the much safer seats.

00:32:07.915 --> 00:32:10.420
And so finally, I just
wanna end with a couple

00:32:10.420 --> 00:32:13.830
of questions that we wanna
pose out into the ether.

00:32:13.830 --> 00:32:15.190
So the first is how should we think

00:32:15.190 --> 00:32:17.130
about nonpartisan democratic aims

00:32:17.130 --> 00:32:19.060
in the context of redistricting?

00:32:19.060 --> 00:32:21.710
Oftentimes the discourse on
gerrymandering is talking

00:32:21.710 --> 00:32:23.770
about partisan gerrymandering

00:32:23.770 --> 00:32:26.500
and goes to this question of
substantive representation

00:32:26.500 --> 00:32:28.200
as Pitkin would put it.

00:32:28.200 --> 00:32:29.260
But what we're really talking

00:32:29.260 --> 00:32:31.050
about is descriptive representation

00:32:31.050 --> 00:32:33.180
and we think that in
line with these findings,

00:32:33.180 --> 00:32:35.800
there are also important
descriptive consequences

00:32:35.800 --> 00:32:37.010
of safe seats through things

00:32:37.010 --> 00:32:39.000
like majority-minority districts,

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:40.130
it's important to think

00:32:40.130 --> 00:32:43.070
about these nonpartisan democratic aims

00:32:43.070 --> 00:32:44.260
when we're considering something

00:32:44.260 --> 00:32:46.260
as partisan as redistricting.

00:32:46.260 --> 00:32:49.280
Our second question is
what we presented here

00:32:49.280 --> 00:32:54.280
today has been entirely
observational evidence.

00:32:54.990 --> 00:32:57.330
So one of the questions that
we have going forward are

00:32:57.330 --> 00:32:59.510
are there ways to gather more experimental

00:32:59.510 --> 00:33:02.520
or quasi-experimental
evidence on this phenomenon?

00:33:02.520 --> 00:33:04.360
And in terms of future research,

00:33:04.360 --> 00:33:05.440
just to set that up,

00:33:05.440 --> 00:33:07.450
one of the outstanding questions is

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:09.270
how do the few women who do win

00:33:09.270 --> 00:33:12.270
in these safe Republican seats
respond to these pressures?

00:33:12.270 --> 00:33:14.160
And we're thinking of recent examples

00:33:14.160 --> 00:33:17.333
of people like Marjorie
Taylor Greene, for example.

00:33:18.430 --> 00:33:21.943
And that brings us to the end
of our prepared presentation.

00:33:26.170 --> 00:33:27.200
- Okay.

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:28.033
All right.

00:33:28.890 --> 00:33:31.410
Thanks very much for the invitation

00:33:31.410 --> 00:33:33.733
to comment on this terrific paper.

00:33:35.150 --> 00:33:37.790
This paper examines
one of the most salient

00:33:37.790 --> 00:33:41.380
and interesting changes in
Congress over recent decades

00:33:41.380 --> 00:33:43.210
and that is the divergence

00:33:43.210 --> 00:33:46.550
in the gender composition
of the two parties.

00:33:46.550 --> 00:33:50.740
Before 1990, women were slowly
increasing their presence

00:33:50.740 --> 00:33:53.200
in both congressional parties,

00:33:53.200 --> 00:33:56.780
but after 1990 the two parties diverged.

00:33:56.780 --> 00:34:00.080
After the so-called year
of the women in 1992,

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:02.360
women steadily and sharply increased

00:34:02.360 --> 00:34:05.400
as a share of the Democratic
Party in Congress.

00:34:05.400 --> 00:34:07.660
By contrast, Republicans stopped becoming

00:34:07.660 --> 00:34:11.800
more gender diverse for
20 years after 2004.

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:14.610
The first significant
increase in Republican women

00:34:14.610 --> 00:34:17.700
just happened with the 2020 elections,

00:34:17.700 --> 00:34:20.880
75% jump over the preceding Congress.

00:34:20.880 --> 00:34:25.340
So 2020 was unquestionably the
year of the Republican women.

00:34:25.340 --> 00:34:28.300
But even after the 2020
boost for Republicans,

00:34:28.300 --> 00:34:31.890
the gender discrepancy between
the parties is still wide.

00:34:31.890 --> 00:34:35.980
Nearly 40% of congressional
Democrats are women,

00:34:35.980 --> 00:34:40.000
as compared to 14% of
congressional Republicans.

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:42.930
This remarkable partisan divergence

00:34:42.930 --> 00:34:46.670
in women's representation is
just one among other forms

00:34:46.670 --> 00:34:50.380
of sociological divergence
between the parties.

00:34:50.380 --> 00:34:52.420
Just for perspective,
I'll highlight a couple

00:34:52.420 --> 00:34:56.170
of other cases of social
sorting in Congress.

00:34:56.170 --> 00:34:59.710
Religion, I'll focus on
evangelical Christians,

00:34:59.710 --> 00:35:02.040
the largest religious group in the US,

00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:05.020
constituting just over a
quarter of the population.

00:35:05.020 --> 00:35:08.170
In the late 1970s,
evangelicals made up roughly

00:35:08.170 --> 00:35:11.300
the same share of both
congressional parties.

00:35:11.300 --> 00:35:14.190
But since the 80s, evangelicals
have steadily sorted

00:35:14.190 --> 00:35:17.750
out of the Democratic Party
and into the Republican Party.

00:35:17.750 --> 00:35:18.940
In recent congresses,

00:35:18.940 --> 00:35:22.380
evangelicals make up more
than 1/3 of Republicans,

00:35:22.380 --> 00:35:24.693
but less than 5% of Democrats.

00:35:26.120 --> 00:35:29.070
Race, another form of social sorting,

00:35:29.070 --> 00:35:32.890
as the US population has
become more racially diverse

00:35:32.890 --> 00:35:34.610
since the 1960s,

00:35:34.610 --> 00:35:37.430
virtually all of that
diversity is represented

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:39.610
in the Democratic Party in Congress.

00:35:39.610 --> 00:35:40.443
The Democratic Party

00:35:40.443 --> 00:35:42.620
in Congress has grown
steadily more racially

00:35:42.620 --> 00:35:44.940
and ethically diverse since the 1970s,

00:35:44.940 --> 00:35:46.800
such that 40% of the party

00:35:46.800 --> 00:35:50.260
in Congress is currently
Black, Hispanic or Asian.

00:35:50.260 --> 00:35:52.650
Meanwhile, the nation's
increasing diversity is

00:35:52.650 --> 00:35:55.390
just passing the Republican Party by.

00:35:55.390 --> 00:35:56.960
Non-whites have never made up more

00:35:56.960 --> 00:36:00.360
than 7% of Republicans in Congress.

00:36:00.360 --> 00:36:04.630
So the parties have been sorting
out in sociological terms.

00:36:04.630 --> 00:36:07.710
The parties are not just
polarized ideologically,

00:36:07.710 --> 00:36:09.820
they're also strongly differentiated

00:36:09.820 --> 00:36:11.763
in terms of personal identity.

00:36:12.880 --> 00:36:15.480
The paper we're discussing
today helps give us

00:36:15.480 --> 00:36:18.160
some new perspective on
factors that are driving

00:36:18.160 --> 00:36:20.513
these changes in
congressional composition.

00:36:21.410 --> 00:36:24.510
The paper argues that
increases in the number

00:36:24.510 --> 00:36:27.660
of safe seats affect the
two parties differently.

00:36:27.660 --> 00:36:29.520
Women candidates are more competitive

00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:31.700
for safe Democratic Party seats

00:36:31.700 --> 00:36:34.850
but less competitive for
safe Republican seats.

00:36:34.850 --> 00:36:37.640
Thus, the rise in safe seats
can help shed some light

00:36:37.640 --> 00:36:40.190
on what's happening in both parties.

00:36:40.190 --> 00:36:42.350
The processes laid out in this paper,

00:36:42.350 --> 00:36:44.360
involves multiple steps.

00:36:44.360 --> 00:36:47.960
The paper theorizes
that seat safety affects

00:36:47.960 --> 00:36:50.070
the decision to run,

00:36:50.070 --> 00:36:51.630
who wins in primaries

00:36:51.630 --> 00:36:54.030
and who wins in the general election.

00:36:54.030 --> 00:36:55.940
Seat safety is thought to affect each

00:36:55.940 --> 00:36:57.733
of these steps in the process.

00:36:58.620 --> 00:37:02.340
As the authors continue
to work on this paper,

00:37:02.340 --> 00:37:06.210
I'd like to see a more careful
breakdown of each step.

00:37:06.210 --> 00:37:09.350
Ideally the paper would nail
down the extent to which each

00:37:09.350 --> 00:37:12.740
of these factors contributes
to the overall pattern.

00:37:12.740 --> 00:37:15.630
It would be great to see
the descriptive statistics,

00:37:15.630 --> 00:37:19.420
you know, even without any
controls as a first order matter.

00:37:19.420 --> 00:37:22.660
What is the difference between
safe Republican districts

00:37:22.660 --> 00:37:25.150
and those that are
marginal for Republicans

00:37:25.150 --> 00:37:28.400
in terms of the number of
women running in the primaries,

00:37:28.400 --> 00:37:30.760
the likelihood that a
woman wins the primary,

00:37:30.760 --> 00:37:33.810
the likelihood that a woman
wins the general election?

00:37:33.810 --> 00:37:35.600
And what are the differences between safe

00:37:35.600 --> 00:37:38.100
Democratic districts and
those that are marginal

00:37:38.100 --> 00:37:39.770
for Democrats on those dimensions

00:37:39.770 --> 00:37:42.163
and what are the effect
sizes at each step?

00:37:43.830 --> 00:37:47.450
My key recommendation
is that I think it'd be

00:37:47.450 --> 00:37:50.250
useful to model the
two parties separately.

00:37:50.250 --> 00:37:52.810
The party works with one overall model,

00:37:52.810 --> 00:37:56.000
assuming that seat safety
affects female candidates

00:37:56.000 --> 00:37:58.250
in both parties in ways
that can be handled

00:37:58.250 --> 00:38:00.900
with simple interaction effects

00:38:00.900 --> 00:38:02.660
but there are reasons to
think that the effects

00:38:02.660 --> 00:38:07.040
of safe seats on candidacy may
differ for the two parties.

00:38:07.040 --> 00:38:08.190
I can think of three ways

00:38:08.190 --> 00:38:10.820
in which gender might affect
congressional elections

00:38:10.820 --> 00:38:11.940
and candidacy.

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:13.920
First, and this is the one that's covered

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:15.700
in most depth in the paper,

00:38:15.700 --> 00:38:18.380
women are perceived as more liberal

00:38:18.380 --> 00:38:21.130
which is a disadvantage in
safe Republican districts

00:38:21.130 --> 00:38:23.763
and an advantage in safe
Democratic districts.

00:38:24.910 --> 00:38:27.290
The paper substantially elaborates on that

00:38:28.167 --> 00:38:29.000
but there are other reasons

00:38:29.000 --> 00:38:31.770
beyond voter perceptions to expect

00:38:31.770 --> 00:38:35.550
that safe seats will affect
gender representation.

00:38:35.550 --> 00:38:38.370
There's perception and
then there's reality.

00:38:38.370 --> 00:38:40.270
Women actually are more liberal,

00:38:40.270 --> 00:38:41.700
even among Republicans,

00:38:41.700 --> 00:38:43.590
such that there is a smaller pool

00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:46.660
of potential female
Republicans considering a run

00:38:46.660 --> 00:38:49.440
for office in conservative districts.

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:52.340
This is something Danielle
Thompson finds in her research

00:38:52.340 --> 00:38:54.080
on state legislatures.

00:38:54.080 --> 00:38:55.200
In an article published

00:38:55.200 --> 00:38:58.460
in Legislative Studies Quarterly in 2015,

00:38:58.460 --> 00:39:00.360
Thompson finds that there's a dearth

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:03.540
of very conservative
women suitable to make

00:39:03.540 --> 00:39:05.670
a competitive bid for Congress

00:39:05.670 --> 00:39:08.240
in those strongly Republican districts.

00:39:08.240 --> 00:39:09.560
She suggests that the pool

00:39:09.560 --> 00:39:11.850
of very conservative women may be growing

00:39:12.820 --> 00:39:17.240
but it was still small
as of her study in 2015.

00:39:17.240 --> 00:39:20.200
There simply weren't many
quality Republican women

00:39:20.200 --> 00:39:23.400
candidates available to run
in conservative districts,

00:39:23.400 --> 00:39:26.630
meaning conservative women
who had won elected office

00:39:26.630 --> 00:39:28.740
at some level before.

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:32.700
But the same pattern doesn't
exist for the Democratic Party.

00:39:32.700 --> 00:39:34.980
Thompson finds that
there's a substantial pool

00:39:34.980 --> 00:39:38.480
of moderate women available
in state legislatures able

00:39:38.480 --> 00:39:39.730
to make a bid for Congress.

00:39:39.730 --> 00:39:42.970
So there's no shortage of
Democratic women available to run

00:39:42.970 --> 00:39:45.373
in both moderate and liberal districts.

00:39:47.120 --> 00:39:48.917
Third, potential women candidates may have

00:39:48.917 --> 00:39:52.500
a differential willingness
to run in the two parties.

00:39:52.500 --> 00:39:54.500
Women may look at the Republican Party

00:39:54.500 --> 00:39:57.850
in Congress and see
themselves not fitting in,

00:39:57.850 --> 00:40:01.360
after all it's an overwhelmingly
male dominated party.

00:40:01.360 --> 00:40:03.900
Women may not look at
the congressional GOP

00:40:03.900 --> 00:40:06.490
and see a caucus in which they feel likely

00:40:06.490 --> 00:40:08.227
that they'll be able to advance

00:40:08.227 --> 00:40:10.850
their personal and policy goals.

00:40:10.850 --> 00:40:12.140
But women probably don't look

00:40:12.140 --> 00:40:14.210
at the Democratic Party similarly.

00:40:14.210 --> 00:40:15.500
They're likely to perceive

00:40:15.500 --> 00:40:17.350
the Congressional Democratic Caucus

00:40:17.350 --> 00:40:19.350
as one where they might fit in

00:40:19.350 --> 00:40:21.893
and be able to advance their personal.

00:40:32.110 --> 00:40:35.650
That's the safe seat's logic
layout in the paper work

00:40:35.650 --> 00:40:38.200
somewhat differently for the two parties.

00:40:38.200 --> 00:40:40.470
Democratic Party is reasonably friendly

00:40:40.470 --> 00:40:43.170
to women candidates across the board.

00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:44.690
Meanwhile, Republicans,

00:40:44.690 --> 00:40:47.300
Republican women probably
face a tougher road

00:40:47.300 --> 00:40:50.750
where they are more disadvantaged
in conservative districts.

00:40:50.750 --> 00:40:54.283
The Democratic women are
disadvantaged in swing districts.

00:40:55.320 --> 00:40:57.650
If these kinds of asymmetries exist,

00:40:57.650 --> 00:40:59.740
the model used in the paper,

00:40:59.740 --> 00:41:01.930
which includes candidates of both parties,

00:41:01.930 --> 00:41:05.310
will likely understate the
gender effect for Republicans

00:41:05.310 --> 00:41:08.760
and overstate the gender
effect for Democrats.

00:41:08.760 --> 00:41:11.750
So I'd like to see a separate
model for the two parties

00:41:11.750 --> 00:41:13.940
and does seat safety
have a significant effect

00:41:13.940 --> 00:41:16.723
on women's candidacies for both parties?

00:41:18.010 --> 00:41:21.430
Second point I'd like to make
is just a note of caution.

00:41:21.430 --> 00:41:22.500
I really think the authors are

00:41:22.500 --> 00:41:24.410
onto something here with this argument

00:41:24.410 --> 00:41:27.600
about how safe seats affect
gender representation

00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:29.390
but it's important to
note that there's a limit

00:41:29.390 --> 00:41:31.703
to how much this logic can explain.

00:41:32.660 --> 00:41:34.700
No doubt there's been a major change

00:41:34.700 --> 00:41:35.940
in congressional elections.

00:41:35.940 --> 00:41:39.070
There are far more safe seats today

00:41:39.070 --> 00:41:43.020
than there were in the late
19th and early 20th centuries.

00:41:43.020 --> 00:41:46.910
So here's a figure from
other work by the authors.

00:41:46.910 --> 00:41:49.320
As is evident here, it
used to be that more

00:41:49.320 --> 00:41:52.350
than 40% of house districts
had elections decided

00:41:52.350 --> 00:41:53.810
in the marginal range

00:41:53.810 --> 00:41:56.080
and now it's around 10%,

00:41:56.080 --> 00:41:59.250
but most of that change
happened a long time ago.

00:41:59.250 --> 00:42:01.610
David Mayhew published his famous paper

00:42:01.610 --> 00:42:04.433
on the vanishing marginals in 1974.

00:42:05.420 --> 00:42:10.420
There has been continued decline
in almost 50 years since,

00:42:10.440 --> 00:42:14.310
but the post-1974 change is less

00:42:14.310 --> 00:42:18.743
than the change that occurred
in the 50 years before 1974.

00:42:19.770 --> 00:42:22.470
Meanwhile, recall that the
gender sorting of the parties

00:42:22.470 --> 00:42:24.960
only got underway after 1990.

00:42:24.960 --> 00:42:26.850
Before 1990, the share of women

00:42:26.850 --> 00:42:29.140
in the two congressional parties was low

00:42:29.140 --> 00:42:30.770
but growing in tandem.

00:42:30.770 --> 00:42:33.550
It's only after 1990 that
we see the divergence

00:42:33.550 --> 00:42:35.433
this paper aims to explain.

00:42:36.410 --> 00:42:40.350
The share in this, the change in the share

00:42:40.350 --> 00:42:45.070
of safe seats since 1992 is
just a few percentage points.

00:42:45.070 --> 00:42:48.400
So it's not a dramatic drop since the 90s.

00:42:48.400 --> 00:42:51.440
Given that seat safety hasn't
changed a lot in recent years,

00:42:51.440 --> 00:42:55.180
safe seats can only explain
part of the gender sorting

00:42:55.180 --> 00:42:56.883
that we've seen since 1990.

00:42:57.800 --> 00:42:59.640
Now I'm persuaded that
the authors are really

00:42:59.640 --> 00:43:00.730
onto something here.

00:43:00.730 --> 00:43:03.210
I suspect that the analysis will hold up

00:43:03.210 --> 00:43:05.120
to further robustness checks,

00:43:05.120 --> 00:43:06.960
including, as I recommend,

00:43:06.960 --> 00:43:08.950
splitting the data by party.

00:43:08.950 --> 00:43:11.100
But clearly there's much more to learn

00:43:11.100 --> 00:43:13.600
about what's driving
congressional parties apart

00:43:13.600 --> 00:43:17.173
along lines of gender and
other sociological factors.

00:43:18.180 --> 00:43:21.810
Before I conclude, I'd like
to make one final small point.

00:43:21.810 --> 00:43:25.760
The paper often implies that
the decline of competition

00:43:25.760 --> 00:43:29.270
in congressional races stems
from partisan gerrymandering.

00:43:29.270 --> 00:43:33.123
This linkage is often
suggested in the popular press.

00:43:34.100 --> 00:43:36.510
But political scientists
working on this subject have

00:43:36.510 --> 00:43:41.510
not been able to document
an effect along these lines.

00:43:42.010 --> 00:43:45.013
Here's a representative
finding in the literature

00:43:45.013 --> 00:43:49.950
from a 2006 JOP paper by
Abramowitz, Alexander and Gunning,

00:43:49.950 --> 00:43:54.923
a paper that's cited in
the paper we're discussing.

00:43:55.829 --> 00:43:58.840
Abramowitz and his
coauthors look at the number

00:43:58.840 --> 00:44:01.200
of safe and competitive house seats before

00:44:01.200 --> 00:44:03.560
and after three recent
rounds of redistricting.

00:44:03.560 --> 00:44:05.900
So you can see from the 1980

00:44:05.900 --> 00:44:09.340
and then the number of safe seats in 1982,

00:44:09.340 --> 00:44:11.980
the number of safe and
competitive seats in 1990

00:44:11.980 --> 00:44:15.720
and then after the redistricting
in 1992 and so forth.

00:44:15.720 --> 00:44:17.290
And they find that redistricting does

00:44:17.290 --> 00:44:19.610
not substantially increase the share

00:44:19.610 --> 00:44:21.150
of safe and competitive seat

00:44:21.150 --> 00:44:23.700
and they conclude that
at the national level,

00:44:23.700 --> 00:44:26.310
redistricting has a negligible impact

00:44:26.310 --> 00:44:28.353
on the competitiveness of house seats,

00:44:29.210 --> 00:44:32.080
The reason why partisan
gerrymandering does not

00:44:34.327 --> 00:44:36.330
have a consistent effect

00:44:36.330 --> 00:44:39.180
on electoral competition is

00:44:39.180 --> 00:44:42.560
that partisan, the goal of
partisan gerrymandering is not

00:44:42.560 --> 00:44:45.310
to maximize seat safety.

00:44:45.310 --> 00:44:47.300
The goal of partisan gerrymandering is

00:44:47.300 --> 00:44:51.000
to maximize the number of
winnable seats for a party.

00:44:51.000 --> 00:44:53.517
Effective gerrymandering
cannot pack too many

00:44:53.517 --> 00:44:57.480
of a party's voters into a single district

00:44:57.480 --> 00:45:00.315
if it wants to win as
many seats as possible.

00:45:00.315 --> 00:45:03.050
Gerrymanderers will often have
to go to their own party's

00:45:03.050 --> 00:45:05.250
incumbents to get them to agree

00:45:05.250 --> 00:45:09.090
to reduce their cushion of
support so as to spread out more

00:45:09.090 --> 00:45:12.300
of their party's voters
into neighboring seats.

00:45:12.300 --> 00:45:15.350
The goal of gerrymandering is
to make as many winnable seats

00:45:15.350 --> 00:45:17.930
as possible but doing
so will tend to depress

00:45:17.930 --> 00:45:21.680
a party's margin of
victory in those districts.

00:45:21.680 --> 00:45:23.140
Now, of course, it's also true

00:45:23.140 --> 00:45:26.820
that partisan gerrymanders may aim to pack

00:45:26.820 --> 00:45:31.000
the opposing party's voters
into as few seats as possible

00:45:31.000 --> 00:45:33.840
and doing this obviously makes
for some very safe seats,

00:45:33.840 --> 00:45:35.820
like the overwhelmingly Democratic

00:45:35.820 --> 00:45:38.060
majority-minority districts.

00:45:38.060 --> 00:45:42.450
But gerrymanderers also try to
correct opposing party voters

00:45:42.450 --> 00:45:43.550
across a lot of seats

00:45:43.550 --> 00:45:46.110
so that the opposing party
doesn't have a very good shot

00:45:46.110 --> 00:45:48.064
at winning any of them.

00:45:48.064 --> 00:45:50.360
The bottom line is that taken together,

00:45:50.360 --> 00:45:52.690
gerrymandering makes some seats safer

00:45:52.690 --> 00:45:55.360
and other seats more competitive.

00:45:55.360 --> 00:45:57.870
The overall effect of gerrymandering

00:45:57.870 --> 00:46:00.480
on competition just tends to wash out.

00:46:00.480 --> 00:46:04.010
The rise of seat safety in
Congress has other causes

00:46:04.010 --> 00:46:06.210
aside from partisan gerrymandering.

00:46:06.210 --> 00:46:08.850
This is not a critical
point for this paper

00:46:08.850 --> 00:46:10.551
given that the paper isn't
even about the causes

00:46:10.551 --> 00:46:12.610
of safe seats anyway,

00:46:12.610 --> 00:46:14.880
but just to steer the authors away

00:46:14.880 --> 00:46:18.310
from unnecessary
controversy in the writeup.

00:46:18.310 --> 00:46:19.663
So I'll stop there.

00:46:24.552 --> 00:46:25.385
- I think that's probably good.

00:46:25.385 --> 00:46:26.250
Everyone see that okay?

00:46:27.700 --> 00:46:30.090
I only have two slides

00:46:30.090 --> 00:46:33.280
and one of them is
actually from the authors.

00:46:33.280 --> 00:46:35.230
Well, I'll start off by
just saying thank you

00:46:35.230 --> 00:46:36.900
and I really enjoyed this opportunity.

00:46:36.900 --> 00:46:38.030
I enjoyed reading the paper.

00:46:38.030 --> 00:46:39.820
I think it's a great topic.

00:46:39.820 --> 00:46:44.450
It's a really useful data exercise

00:46:44.450 --> 00:46:45.283
and they're just some really

00:46:45.283 --> 00:46:48.240
interesting stylized facts
that are starting to emerge.

00:46:48.240 --> 00:46:50.450
I think the first time
I read through the paper

00:46:50.450 --> 00:46:54.450
I felt, you know, like
I was kind of convinced

00:46:54.450 --> 00:46:55.640
by what the authors were saying

00:46:55.640 --> 00:46:58.410
and wasn't sure what I would have to say

00:46:58.410 --> 00:47:01.020
but then on looking at it more carefully,

00:47:01.020 --> 00:47:02.570
I started to have a lot more questions

00:47:02.570 --> 00:47:04.577
and some of them are about the theory

00:47:04.577 --> 00:47:06.270
and some of them are about the empirics

00:47:06.270 --> 00:47:09.840
and I'll dive into both of those.

00:47:09.840 --> 00:47:13.200
First thing I wanna do is
just to say a little bit

00:47:13.200 --> 00:47:16.050
about the ways of framing the paper.

00:47:16.050 --> 00:47:17.793
I mean, obviously there's a lot,

00:47:18.750 --> 00:47:20.840
you have some some folks among you,

00:47:20.840 --> 00:47:22.560
among the authors who know a lot

00:47:22.560 --> 00:47:25.870
about the cross national
literature on gender

00:47:25.870 --> 00:47:27.220
and representation

00:47:27.220 --> 00:47:30.910
and maybe linking this in
with a larger literature

00:47:30.910 --> 00:47:34.487
on gender and, you know,
why the US perhaps is,

00:47:34.487 --> 00:47:37.730
does this paper help us
understand why the US is

00:47:37.730 --> 00:47:39.770
an outlier relative to other countries,

00:47:39.770 --> 00:47:42.420
might be an interesting
framing device to consider.

00:47:42.420 --> 00:47:45.290
But also in the US literature,

00:47:45.290 --> 00:47:46.830
one paper I wanted to
direct your attention

00:47:46.830 --> 00:47:48.340
to is by a former student of mine,

00:47:48.340 --> 00:47:51.760
Sarah Anzia has a paper with
using a really large data set

00:47:51.760 --> 00:47:53.620
from California in which she finds kind

00:47:53.620 --> 00:47:56.670
of surprising evidence of a
advantage for female candidates

00:47:56.670 --> 00:47:58.530
in a lot of local offices

00:47:58.530 --> 00:48:01.480
and finds that there's
something about the nature

00:48:01.480 --> 00:48:03.870
of those offices that advantages largest

00:48:03.870 --> 00:48:04.930
for school board races

00:48:04.930 --> 00:48:07.600
and it gets a little smaller
for city council races

00:48:07.600 --> 00:48:10.040
and a little smaller
for mayoral elections,

00:48:10.040 --> 00:48:11.430
but she also finds some evidence

00:48:11.430 --> 00:48:12.410
that's consistent with yours,

00:48:12.410 --> 00:48:15.570
that says that this basically disappears,

00:48:15.570 --> 00:48:17.530
this female advantage disappears

00:48:17.530 --> 00:48:19.820
the more conservative
the districts become,

00:48:19.820 --> 00:48:22.330
which is perhaps not too surprising.

00:48:22.330 --> 00:48:23.940
I also think there's a related literature

00:48:23.940 --> 00:48:25.420
on local governments in the US,

00:48:25.420 --> 00:48:27.200
it might be nice to also think about,

00:48:27.200 --> 00:48:30.650
where the story is that one emerging story

00:48:30.650 --> 00:48:33.180
and it's one that I've seen
in some data I've looked at,

00:48:33.180 --> 00:48:35.140
is that when we look
at at-large districts,

00:48:35.140 --> 00:48:37.970
versus when the same
people are represented

00:48:37.970 --> 00:48:38.830
in the at-large districts

00:48:38.830 --> 00:48:41.960
versus single member district elections,

00:48:41.960 --> 00:48:46.110
the at-large districts are
better for female recruitment.

00:48:46.110 --> 00:48:47.780
We end up with more
female candidates running

00:48:47.780 --> 00:48:49.410
and more female candidates winning

00:48:49.410 --> 00:48:51.110
and that probably has something to do

00:48:51.110 --> 00:48:54.630
with competition
competitiveness in, you know,

00:48:54.630 --> 00:48:58.990
in winner take all, municipal
city council elections are

00:48:58.990 --> 00:49:00.520
often very uncompetitive

00:49:00.520 --> 00:49:02.919
and there are long-standing
incumbents hanging around

00:49:02.919 --> 00:49:07.790
who are, these are people who
a female candidate might be

00:49:07.790 --> 00:49:11.040
less inclined to jump
in and try to challenge

00:49:11.040 --> 00:49:14.280
than maybe, say a seven
member school board

00:49:14.280 --> 00:49:17.130
that has three seats up,
three at-large seats up,

00:49:17.130 --> 00:49:19.830
two of which might be
open seats effectively.

00:49:19.830 --> 00:49:21.240
That's a very different kind of setting.

00:49:21.240 --> 00:49:23.700
So this kind of literature
I think is very related

00:49:23.700 --> 00:49:26.233
to what you're doing in
the congressional context.

00:49:27.550 --> 00:49:28.990
But I would also encourage you to think,

00:49:28.990 --> 00:49:30.260
you know, you've got several authors

00:49:30.260 --> 00:49:31.720
and hopefully a lot of time on your hands,

00:49:31.720 --> 00:49:35.720
maybe it would be a very
small lift to add state

00:49:35.720 --> 00:49:37.163
legislatures to this study.

00:49:41.820 --> 00:49:43.820
This Carl Clark dataset

00:49:43.820 --> 00:49:46.910
on state legislatures could
be linked with the DIME data

00:49:46.910 --> 00:49:48.940
and you could be up and
running doing some similar kind

00:49:48.940 --> 00:49:51.223
of analysis with state legislatures

00:49:51.223 --> 00:49:52.450
and that would just
give you a larger sample

00:49:52.450 --> 00:49:54.750
and some interesting
cross-state heterogeneity

00:49:54.750 --> 00:49:56.220
and all of that.

00:49:56.220 --> 00:50:00.370
So that would be a nice
thing to think about doing.

00:50:00.370 --> 00:50:04.730
So one thing that I thought
Frances might get into,

00:50:04.730 --> 00:50:06.990
so she definitely checked the
box on the gerrymandering,

00:50:06.990 --> 00:50:10.180
so everything she said about
gerrymandering, I agree with.

00:50:10.180 --> 00:50:12.170
I think it's good, it's
good to have, you know,

00:50:12.170 --> 00:50:14.210
when people, two people
have the same reaction,

00:50:14.210 --> 00:50:15.720
it's good to know what that is.

00:50:15.720 --> 00:50:17.980
I don't think this is a paper
about gerrymandering per se.

00:50:17.980 --> 00:50:19.340
It's a paper about safe seats

00:50:19.340 --> 00:50:21.030
and so you don't need
to make those arguments

00:50:21.030 --> 00:50:22.670
about gerrymandering,

00:50:22.670 --> 00:50:25.763
but it's something that I
thought might come up is just,

00:50:25.763 --> 00:50:28.510
I was surprised not to see incumbency

00:50:28.510 --> 00:50:30.340
at least among the control variables,

00:50:30.340 --> 00:50:33.970
even more, I thought it might make sense

00:50:33.970 --> 00:50:37.310
as something to explore
heterogeneous effects, you know,

00:50:37.310 --> 00:50:41.020
are we talking about what happens
when we look at open seats

00:50:41.020 --> 00:50:43.080
versus seats where there are
long-standing incumbents?

00:50:43.080 --> 00:50:46.810
You know, I think that
incumbency of course is

00:50:46.810 --> 00:50:50.206
highly correlated with
the safety of the seats

00:50:50.206 --> 00:50:52.000
and a lot of those safe
seats are gonna be ones

00:50:52.000 --> 00:50:54.837
when there is an incumbent,
longstanding incumbent running

00:50:54.837 --> 00:50:56.260
and some of the people in your data set,

00:50:56.260 --> 00:50:58.090
you're looking at some of
these challengers are gonna be

00:50:58.090 --> 00:51:00.050
not particularly serious challengers

00:51:00.050 --> 00:51:01.970
because it's someone who
everyone knows is gonna

00:51:01.970 --> 00:51:04.640
win both the primary and the general

00:51:04.640 --> 00:51:08.040
and then there are situations though

00:51:08.040 --> 00:51:11.020
even in a very, even in a very safe seat

00:51:11.020 --> 00:51:13.590
where a longstanding incumbent steps down

00:51:13.590 --> 00:51:15.300
and then there's an open seat.

00:51:15.300 --> 00:51:18.180
Surely the dynamics are different in those

00:51:18.180 --> 00:51:22.573
and so it'd be useful to take
a look at those separately.

00:51:23.990 --> 00:51:27.760
I also wanna think about maybe, of course,

00:51:27.760 --> 00:51:30.930
you know, I have to say something
about political geography

00:51:30.930 --> 00:51:33.687
and think about how that might matter.

00:51:33.687 --> 00:51:37.682
One possible alternative hypothesis is,

00:51:37.682 --> 00:51:38.730
you know, is if you just
look at the correlation

00:51:38.730 --> 00:51:41.520
between the Cook Index
and population density,

00:51:41.520 --> 00:51:43.190
the correlation is like, you know,

00:51:43.190 --> 00:51:45.693
it's like .9 or something, it's very high.

00:51:47.410 --> 00:51:48.990
This thing, you know,

00:51:48.990 --> 00:51:50.530
the main independent variable here

00:51:50.530 --> 00:51:53.440
or the interaction term
is very much correlated

00:51:53.440 --> 00:51:56.250
with geography in a particular way

00:51:56.250 --> 00:51:58.210
so that the safe seats are essentially,

00:51:58.210 --> 00:52:00.450
safe Republican seats are very rural,

