measure of margin of victory,

01:03:45.980 --> 01:03:47.860
you don't really see a lot of difference

01:03:47.860 --> 01:03:49.550
in the last 20 or 30 years

01:03:49.550 --> 01:03:52.470
but I think this measure
in general is very kinda,

01:03:52.470 --> 01:03:53.690
not very robust, right?

01:03:53.690 --> 01:03:56.570
That's why I feel comfortable
plotting it starting right

01:03:56.570 --> 01:03:58.430
after the Civil War in these, in general,

01:03:58.430 --> 01:03:59.840
you see this kind of downward trend

01:03:59.840 --> 01:04:01.620
but I think if you kind of want to zoom in

01:04:01.620 --> 01:04:03.647
and look at the trajectory
within 20 or 30 years,

01:04:03.647 --> 01:04:07.270
the thing it's not as easy to
do that because of, you know,

01:04:07.270 --> 01:04:09.480
different, the redistricting

01:04:09.480 --> 01:04:11.950
and other also changes that have occurred.

01:04:11.950 --> 01:04:13.310
I think that's why I think

01:04:13.310 --> 01:04:15.850
the Cook Partisan Voting
Index is more robust

01:04:15.850 --> 01:04:18.420
as a measure of
ex-anticompetitiveness, right?

01:04:18.420 --> 01:04:20.870
So where we look at the
partisan composition

01:04:20.870 --> 01:04:22.420
in the two elections prior,

01:04:22.420 --> 01:04:25.270
relative to the, you
know, nation in general.

01:04:25.270 --> 01:04:27.770
I think if you look at the
Cook Partisan Voting Index

01:04:27.770 --> 01:04:29.700
and the trajectory in the last 30 years,

01:04:29.700 --> 01:04:31.259
starting in 1990,

01:04:31.259 --> 01:04:32.092
we actually we have this evidence

01:04:32.092 --> 01:04:35.920
that safe seats have
been on decrease, right?

01:04:35.920 --> 01:04:38.310
Pretty, you know, steadily, right,

01:04:38.310 --> 01:04:39.740
it's not a huge change

01:04:39.740 --> 01:04:41.950
and unfortunately don't
really have a lot of,

01:04:41.950 --> 01:04:43.990
I think it's possible to construct it

01:04:43.990 --> 01:04:45.040
but we don't really have this data at hand

01:04:45.040 --> 01:04:47.870
in regard to Cook PVI prior to 1990

01:04:47.870 --> 01:04:50.360
but I believe if we did
have it probably would see

01:04:50.360 --> 01:04:54.580
that it's been in decline
since before that as well.

01:04:54.580 --> 01:04:58.760
- Okay, Before Akhil takes
up Jonathan's question about

01:04:58.760 --> 01:05:00.350
figure two,

01:05:00.350 --> 01:05:02.753
I see we do have a question from Gail.

01:05:04.890 --> 01:05:07.740
- [Gail] So I just joined
your session a little bit late

01:05:07.740 --> 01:05:10.714
but my question is,

01:05:10.714 --> 01:05:14.830
because this discussion
has revolved around women

01:05:16.990 --> 01:05:20.650
seeking candidacy in safe seats.

01:05:20.650 --> 01:05:23.318
I just wanted to get your impression

01:05:23.318 --> 01:05:26.510
on the Republican Party's attempt to,

01:05:26.510 --> 01:05:31.060
attempt on voter suppression
and gerrymandering.

01:05:31.060 --> 01:05:35.240
What impact do you think this
will have on the potential

01:05:35.240 --> 01:05:38.973
for women running for
congressional offices?

01:05:43.890 --> 01:05:46.299
- So while you think about that,

01:05:46.299 --> 01:05:49.123
why don't we get Natalie's
question as well.

01:05:56.001 --> 01:05:57.203
- [Natalie] Hello, can you hear me?

01:05:58.070 --> 01:05:58.903
Hi.

01:05:58.903 --> 01:06:00.540
So I'm new to all of this

01:06:00.540 --> 01:06:02.420
and I'm just a high school senior

01:06:02.420 --> 01:06:04.440
and I'm not even sure if I'm understanding

01:06:04.440 --> 01:06:06.163
all of this right but.

01:06:09.260 --> 01:06:10.170
Hello?

01:06:10.170 --> 01:06:11.520
- Yeah, you've come back, yeah.

01:06:11.520 --> 01:06:12.353
- [Natalie] Hi.

01:06:12.353 --> 01:06:16.210
So I'm wondering if the
person who is responsible

01:06:16.210 --> 01:06:19.510
for gerrymandering is
the state legislatures

01:06:19.510 --> 01:06:22.760
and that means that they are allowed

01:06:22.760 --> 01:06:25.000
to increase or decrease safe seats

01:06:25.000 --> 01:06:27.990
and competitive seats in like a district.

01:06:27.990 --> 01:06:31.004
Does that mean that state legislatures

01:06:31.004 --> 01:06:33.230
in theory can manipulate

01:06:33.230 --> 01:06:37.403
like female representative in an area?

01:06:39.430 --> 01:06:41.110
Yeah, that's my question.

01:06:41.110 --> 01:06:42.770
Thank you.

01:06:42.770 --> 01:06:43.650
- Yeah. Thank you.

01:06:43.650 --> 01:06:45.610
I think presumably in theory

01:06:45.610 --> 01:06:47.070
and that's, I think that's one,

01:06:47.070 --> 01:06:48.460
actually just look at the paper

01:06:48.460 --> 01:06:50.320
and I think that's one of the,

01:06:50.320 --> 01:06:52.930
we use the word gerrymandering
twice in the paper

01:06:52.930 --> 01:06:54.040
and I think that's one of the kind

01:06:54.040 --> 01:06:55.747
of policy suggestions that we have

01:06:55.747 --> 01:06:58.670
and that's what discussants
were not really happy about.

01:06:58.670 --> 01:07:01.500
So I don't think that, so
in theory it's possible

01:07:01.500 --> 01:07:03.060
but I think in practice, you know,

01:07:03.060 --> 01:07:05.170
due to kind of partisan
sorting more generally

01:07:05.170 --> 01:07:07.830
that people just tend to
live with someone who is

01:07:07.830 --> 01:07:10.750
more similar to them
ideology, whether, you know,

01:07:10.750 --> 01:07:13.300
deliberately or not, it
happens that, you know,

01:07:13.300 --> 01:07:16.420
Republicans live in rural
areas with other Republicans

01:07:16.420 --> 01:07:20.100
and Democrats live in urban
areas with other Democrats.

01:07:20.100 --> 01:07:21.940
So, you know, the extent
to which you can kind

01:07:21.940 --> 01:07:23.900
of manufacture, you know,

01:07:23.900 --> 01:07:25.210
safe seats with gerrymandering

01:07:25.210 --> 01:07:26.390
I think is very limited

01:07:26.390 --> 01:07:28.880
but you can imagine how it is possible

01:07:28.880 --> 01:07:30.670
and then thereby affect, you know,

01:07:30.670 --> 01:07:32.550
the proportion of female representation.

01:07:32.550 --> 01:07:35.400
But I don't think that's a big concern

01:07:35.400 --> 01:07:37.790
and I think the same kind
of answer I would have

01:07:37.790 --> 01:07:39.750
with regard to voter
suppression, which is,

01:07:39.750 --> 01:07:42.320
you know, a very important
issue but I'm not sure how

01:07:42.320 --> 01:07:45.030
exactly it would play out with
regard to the gender dynamic

01:07:45.030 --> 01:07:47.483
if any of my colleagues would like to add

01:07:47.483 --> 01:07:49.823
on that it would be great.

01:07:51.630 --> 01:07:53.911
- I would just, I would just add

01:07:53.911 --> 01:07:57.920
that whatever the causes
of safe seats are,

01:07:57.920 --> 01:08:00.440
to what extent it's self sorting

01:08:00.440 --> 01:08:02.650
or what extent it's demography,

01:08:02.650 --> 01:08:04.990
to what extent it's gerrymandering

01:08:04.990 --> 01:08:07.140
and to what extent it's things

01:08:07.140 --> 01:08:09.023
like majority-minority districts,

01:08:09.890 --> 01:08:12.940
I don't think to the
extent there's any agency

01:08:12.940 --> 01:08:15.520
involved, human agency and planning,

01:08:15.520 --> 01:08:18.790
I don't think anybody was intending it

01:08:18.790 --> 01:08:21.220
to reduce female representation

01:08:21.220 --> 01:08:23.430
the Republican side relative
to the Democratic side.

01:08:23.430 --> 01:08:25.780
I think it's an unintended side effect

01:08:26.738 --> 01:08:29.883
of whatever has been done
by people, that's my guess.

01:08:31.920 --> 01:08:33.740
- Yeah and just to jump in there,

01:08:33.740 --> 01:08:35.470
cause I think, again,

01:08:35.470 --> 01:08:39.340
taking the comments from our
two discussants very seriously,

01:08:39.340 --> 01:08:42.230
I think there is a
question, at least for me,

01:08:42.230 --> 01:08:43.700
which is that when we look at things

01:08:43.700 --> 01:08:45.060
like majority-minority districts,

01:08:45.060 --> 01:08:49.880
we do see examples of
intentional map manipulation,

01:08:49.880 --> 01:08:51.132
if you wanna call it that,

01:08:51.132 --> 01:08:54.110
to advance descriptive representation.

01:08:54.110 --> 01:08:56.398
So I think one of the points
that we were trying to make

01:08:56.398 --> 01:08:58.640
with our references to gerrymandering,

01:08:58.640 --> 01:09:00.210
which noted should be taken out,

01:09:00.210 --> 01:09:02.430
is not necessarily that
that is the cause right now

01:09:02.430 --> 01:09:04.600
but that could be a prerogative

01:09:04.600 --> 01:09:06.090
that gerrymanderers think about

01:09:06.090 --> 01:09:07.640
or people drawing the lines

01:09:07.640 --> 01:09:09.060
and I think about this in the context of,

01:09:09.060 --> 01:09:10.990
for example, the ACLU right now,

01:09:10.990 --> 01:09:13.460
when they're looking at
redistricting for the next year,

01:09:13.460 --> 01:09:16.520
they're looking at racial
redistricting, for example.

01:09:16.520 --> 01:09:19.670
We don't have a similar concept
for gender redistricting

01:09:19.670 --> 01:09:21.850
but perhaps that's something
that in the back of our minds

01:09:21.850 --> 01:09:23.160
we should keep in mind,

01:09:23.160 --> 01:09:25.460
these unintentional consequences

01:09:25.460 --> 01:09:27.603
that Professor Shapiro was talking about.

01:09:30.000 --> 01:09:33.470
- Okay, I see that we're coming up to 8:15

01:09:33.470 --> 01:09:36.130
which is when we're,

01:09:36.130 --> 01:09:38.650
when we're scheduled to finish.

01:09:38.650 --> 01:09:42.040
I just wanna ask first the discussants,

01:09:42.040 --> 01:09:45.680
are there any last
comments you'd like to make

01:09:45.680 --> 01:09:47.770
and then I'll turn to the panelists

01:09:47.770 --> 01:09:49.920
and ask if there are any last comments

01:09:49.920 --> 01:09:52.700
that any of the panelists
who are presenting

01:09:52.700 --> 01:09:54.743
their work would like to make.

01:09:56.360 --> 01:09:57.500
- Not from me.

01:09:57.500 --> 01:09:58.850
- Okay, thank you, Frances.

01:10:00.370 --> 01:10:01.560
- Just keep up the good work.

01:10:01.560 --> 01:10:04.390
I look forward to seeing
the next draft of this.

01:10:04.390 --> 01:10:05.605
- Terrific.

01:10:05.605 --> 01:10:06.760
Thank you, Jonathan.

